                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "never",
                  "marks": [
                    "italic"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " occur as the ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "y",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "-intercept for any of these line segments.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1$ and every prime number.\n\nEvery obtainable intercept is a product of two integers greater than $1$, so the obtainable integers are exactly the composite numbers.",
          "worked": "From part (a), every intercept has value $n=ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers greater than $1$. Thus $n$ is composite.\n\nConversely, every positive composite integer has a factorisation $n=ab$ with integers $a,b>1$, so it occurs as an intercept.\n\nTherefore the positive integers that never occur are $1$ and all prime numbers.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
